# Promoted from the learning loop (2026-09-19), reviewed and kept — but with the
# overfit branch removed. The loop's original rule also matched corpus-specific
# hostnames (ACME-/FIN-...-SRVn), which memorised the example rather than a
# concept; that branch is dropped. What remains is the general, durable signal:
# an RFC1918 / TEST-NET internal address leaking into committed code/config.
id: KEUR-CBI-001
title: Internal (RFC1918 / TEST-NET) IP address in committed code
category: cbi
severity: warning
engine: pattern
skip_comments: true
target:
  extensions: .js .ts .py .rb .php .go .java .env .yml .yaml .json .md
patterns:
  - regex: \b(?:10\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.168\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[0-1])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.0\.2\.\d{1,3})\b
    message: "Internal IP address (RFC1918 / TEST-NET) in committed code or config (CBI). Prefer a hostname or config value; leaking internal topology aids reconnaissance."
confidence: 0.6
fix: "Replace the hardcoded internal address with a configurable hostname or environment value."
